00:19:37,311 --> 00:19:40,829
in hospitals, commercial and industrial pipefitting,

571
00:19:40,829 --> 00:19:44,076
steamfitting and backflow testing can raise pay.

572
00:19:44,256 --> 00:19:47,385
Service plumbers who handle emergencies and work

573
00:19:47,385 --> 00:19:49,994
on commission in some companies can also

574
00:19:49,994 --> 00:19:51,558
earn well above average.

575
00:19:51,909 --> 00:19:56,050
Specialties usually require extra training and certifications,

576
00:19:56,050 --> 00:19:58,455
but they often come with higher pay,

577
00:19:58,455 --> 00:20:01,127
more stable demand and less competition.

578
00:20:01,307 --> 00:20:04,344
A tradesperson who adds one valuable certification

579
00:20:04,344 --> 00:20:06,956
every few years keeps raising their earning

580
00:20:06,956 --> 00:20:07,321
power.

581
00:20:07,671 --> 00:20:10,429
Let's imagine three electricians who finished their

582
00:20:10,429 --> 00:20:11,781
apprenticeships together.

583
00:20:11,961 --> 00:20:14,341
The first stays a union journeyman for

584
00:20:14,341 --> 00:20:15,407
his whole career,

585
00:20:15,407 --> 00:20:18,163
works steady hours with occasional overtime,

586
00:20:18,163 --> 00:20:20,858
invests in his retirement plan and pension,

587
00:20:20,858 --> 00:20:23,176
and retires comfortably at sixty two.

588
00:20:23,356 --> 00:20:25,515
No drama, just consistency.

589
00:20:25,866 --> 00:20:28,898
The second moves into industrial controls,

590
00:20:28,898 --> 00:20:30,992
earns several certifications,

591
00:20:30,992 --> 00:20:32,797
and becomes a supervisor.

592
00:20:32,977 --> 00:20:35,537
Her pay climbs well above the median,

593
00:20:35,537 --> 00:20:38,306
and she manages crews on large projects.

594
00:20:38,486 --> 00:20:40,026
She doesn't own a company,

595
00:20:40,026 --> 00:20:42,099
but her specialty makes her hard to

596
00:20:42,099 --> 00:20:42,572
replace.

597
00:20:42,923 --> 00:20:45,920
The third starts a small residential electrical

598
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:47,323
company at thirty two.

599
00:20:47,503 --> 00:20:49,402
The first years are stressful,

600
00:20:49,402 --> 00:20:51,555
with long hours and uneven income.

601
00:20:51,735 --> 00:20:52,911
But by forty five,

602
00:20:52,911 --> 00:20:54,413
he has eight employees,

603
00:20:54,413 --> 00:20:56,635
a steady stream of customers and a

604
00:20:56,635 --> 00:20:58,921
business worth a meaningful amount.

605
00:20:59,101 --> 00:21:00,374
He took the most risk,

606
00:21:00,374 --> 00:21:02,689
and he has the highest potential reward.

607
00:21:03,039 --> 00:21:04,691
Three different paths,

608
00:21:04,691 --> 00:21:05,442
all solid.

609
00:21:05,622 --> 00:21:07,734
That's one of the best things about

610
00:21:07,734 --> 00:21:09,966
the trades: you can choose stability,

611
00:21:09,966 --> 00:21:11,655
specialization or ownership,

612
00:21:11,655 --> 00:21:13,706
and each can lead to a comfortable

613
00:21:13,706 --> 00:21:16,059
financial life if you manage your money

614
00:21:16,059 --> 00:21:16,361
well.

615
00:21:16,711 --> 00:21:19,787
Earning well doesn't automatically mean building wealth.

616
00:21:19,967 --> 00:21:22,327
Here are common money mistakes in the

617
00:21:22,327 --> 00:21:24,240
trades, and how to avoid them.

618
00:21:24,590 --> 00:21:25,553
Mistake one.

619
00:21:25,733 --> 00:21:27,080
The big truck payment.

620
00:21:27,260 --> 00:21:30,047
It's common to see young tradespeople with

621
00:21:30,047 --> 00:21:33,100
expensive new trucks and big monthly payments.

622
00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:35,136
A truck you need for work is

623
00:21:35,136 --> 00:21:35,799
one thing.

624
00:21:35,979 --> 00:21:38,410
A luxury truck financed for seven years

625
00:21:38,410 --> 00:21:40,654
can drain money that could have been

626
00:21:40,654 --> 00:21:41,215
invested.

627
00:21:41,566 --> 00:21:42,622
Mistake two.

628
00:21:42,802 --> 00:21:45,217
Spending overtime as if it's permanent.

629
00:21:45,397 --> 00:21:48,300
Overtime comes and goes with busy seasons

630
00:21:48,300 --> 00:21:49,716
and economic cycles.

631
00:21:49,896 --> 00:21:53,512
Smart tradespeople build their lifestyle on base

632
00:21:53,512 --> 00:21:56,223
pay and save or invest the overtime.

633
00:21:56,403 --> 00:21:57,413
Mistake three.

634
00:21:57,593 --> 00:21:58,963
Not planning for the body.

635
00:21:59,143 --> 00:22:01,310
Physical work gets harder with age,

636
00:22:01,310 --> 00:22:03,847
so save aggressively in your twenties and

637
00:22:03,847 --> 00:22:06,137
thirties and plan for a less physical

638
00:22:06,137 --> 00:22:06,818
role later.

639
00:22:07,168 --> 00:22:08,085
Mistake four.

640
00:22:08,265 --> 00:22:10,273
Ignoring retirement accounts.

641
00:22:10,453 --> 00:22:12,862
If your employer or union offers a

642
00:22:12,862 --> 00:22:15,979
retirement plan with matching contributions,

643
00:22:15,979 --> 00:22:17,396
take full advantage.

644
00:22:17,576 --> 00:22:19,311
And if you're self employed,

645
00:22:19,311 --> 00:22:22,471
look into retirement accounts designed for business

646
00:22:22,471 --> 00:22:22,905
owners.

647
00:22:23,085 --> 00:22:24,072
Mistake five.

648
00:22:24,252 --> 00:22:25,503
No emergency fund,

649
00:22:25,503 --> 00:22:28,006
which matters even more in work that

650
00:22:28,006 --> 00:22:29,952
can be seasonal or cyclical.

651
00:22:30,303 --> 00:22:32,236
If you're starting an apprenticeship,

652
00:22:32,236 --> 00:22:33,646
here's a simple money plan.

653
00:22:33,826 --> 00:22:36,528
First, build a starter emergency fund of

654
00:22:36,528 --> 00:22:37,946
one thousand dollars,

655
00:22:37,946 --> 00:22:40,377
then work toward three to six months

656
00:22:40,377 --> 00:22:41,187
of expenses.

657
00:22:41,367 --> 00:22:44,845
Second, contribute enough to any retirement plan

658
00:22:44,845 --> 00:22:47,237
to get the full employer or union

659
00:22:47,237 --> 00:22:47,671
match.

660
00:22:48,022 --> 00:22:50,293
Third, avoid big debt early,

661
00:22:50,293 --> 00:22:53,294
especially a car or truck payment you

662
00:22:53,294 --> 00:22:54,187
don't need.

663
00:22:54,367 --> 00:22:56,665
Buy reliable and used if you can.

664
00:22:56,845 --> 00:22:59,134
Fourth, every time you get a raise,

665
00:22:59,134 --> 00:23:00,770
save at least half of it.

666
00:23:00,950 --> 00:23:02,805
Your lifestyle grows a little,

667
00:23:02,805 --> 00:23:04,537
and your savings grow a lot.

668
00:23:04,887 --> 00:23:08,003
Fifth, invest consistently in low cost index

669
00:23:08,003 --> 00:23:10,483
funds through a retirement account.

670
00:23:10,663 --> 00:23:13,702
Starting at nineteen or twenty gives compounding

671
00:23:13,702 --> 00:23:14,715
decades to work.

672
00:23:14,895 --> 00:23:17,279
Follow this plan through a four year

673
00:23:17,279 --> 00:23:20,127
apprenticeship, and you could finish with a

674
00:23:20,127 --> 00:23:21,518
solid emergency fund,

675
00:23:21,518 --> 00:23:24,233
a growing retirement account and no debt,

676
00:23:24,233 --> 00:23:26,750
before many of your friends even start

677
00:23:26,750 --> 00:23:27,677
their careers.

678
00:23:28,027 --> 00:23:29,671
Here's what that could look like in

679
00:23:29,671 --> 00:23:30,047
numbers.

680
00:23:30,227 --> 00:23:33,098
Say an apprentice invests three hundred dollars

681
00:23:33,098 --> 00:23:35,236
a month from age nineteen to twenty

682
00:23:35,236 --> 00:23:38,168
three, about fourteen thousand dollars in total.

683
00:23:38,348 --> 00:23:40,124
Then they never add another dollar.

684
00:23:40,304 --> 00:23:42,884
At an illustrative seven percent a year,

685
00:23:42,884 --> 00:23:45,077
that money could grow to more than

686
00:23:45,077 --> 00:23:47,721
two hundred thousand dollars by age sixty

687
00:23:47,721 --> 00:23:47,979
two.

688
00:23:48,159 --> 00:23:51,084
Four years of discipline during an apprenticeship

689
00:23:51,084 --> 00:23:52,935
can quietly fund a big piece of

690
00:23:52,935 --> 00:23:53,592
retirement.

691
00:23:53,942 --> 00:23:56,053
And if they keep investing a modest

692
00:23:56,053 --> 00:23:57,863
amount for their whole career,

693
00:23:57,863 --> 00:23:59,673
the total could be far larger,

694
00:23:59,673 --> 00:24:01,965
especially with an employer match or a

695
00:24:01,965 --> 00:24:02,870
pension on top.

696
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:05,556
The trades provide the income and the

697
00:24:05,556 --> 00:24:06,301
head start.

698
00:24:06,481 --> 00:24:08,787
The money habits turn that head start

699
00:24:08,787 --> 00:24:09,847
into real wealth.

700
00:24:10,198 --> 00:24:11,823
Now, three angles.

701
00:24:12,003 --> 00:24:13,361
First, the numbers.

702
00:24:13,541 --> 00:24:16,279
Let's compare two people over a lifetime,

703
00:24:16,279 --> 00:24:18,348
using simplified illustrations.

704
00:24:18,698 --> 00:24:20,544
Two eighteen year olds.

705
00:24:20,724 --> 00:24:23,185
One apprentice, one college student.

706
00:24:23,365 --> 00:24:24,241
By twenty two,

707
00:24:24,241 --> 00:24:26,805
the apprentice has earned and saved while

708
00:24:26,805 --> 00:24:28,369
the student has borrowed.

709
00:24:28,719 --> 00:24:30,843
Meet two eighteen year olds.

710
00:24:31,023 --> 00:24:33,698
Alex becomes an electrical apprentice,

711
00:24:33,698 --> 00:24:36,302
earning a modest wage that rises each

712
00:24:36,302 --> 00:24:36,654
year.

713
00:24:36,834 --> 00:24:39,087
Sam goes to college and graduates at

714
00:24:39,087 --> 00:24:41,716
twenty two with thirty thousand dollars in

715
00:24:41,716 --> 00:24:42,593
student loans.

716
00:24:42,773 --> 00:24:43,868
Over four years,

717
00:24:43,868 --> 00:24:46,675
Alex earns well over one hundred thousand

718
00:24:46,675 --> 00:24:48,113
dollars in total and,

719
00:24:48,113 --> 00:24:50,851
living with family and saving carefully,

720
00:24:50,851 --> 00:24:53,384
invests twenty five thousand dollars.

721
00:24:53,734 --> 00:24:54,614
At twenty two,

722
00:24:54,614 --> 00:24:57,318
Alex has about twenty five thousand dollars

723
00:24:57,318 --> 00:24:59,771
invested and a nearly finished license.

724
00:24:59,951 --> 00:25:01,113
Sam has a degree,

725
00:25:01,113 --> 00:25:03,642
a new job and thirty thousand dollars

726
00:25:03,642 --> 00:25:04,189
of debt.

727
00:25:04,369 --> 00:25:06,774
That's a fifty five thousand dollar gap

728
00:25:06,774 --> 00:25:09,057
in net worth before Sam's career even

729
00:25:09,057 --> 00:25:09,489
begins.

730
00:25:09,839 --> 00:25:11,499
Now let compounding work.

731
00:25:11,679 --> 00:25:14,445
If Alex's twenty five thousand dollars grows

732
00:25:14,445 --> 00:25:16,897
at an illustrative seven percent a year

733
00:25:16,897 --> 00:25:18,155
until age sixty two,

734
00:25:18,155 --> 00:25:20,921
it could become around three hundred seventy

735
00:25:20,921 --> 00:25:23,939
thousand dollars, without adding another dollar.

736
00:25:24,119 --> 00:25:27,045
Meanwhile, Sam spends years paying off the

737
00:25:27,045 --> 00:25:29,274
loan before investing seriously.

738
00:25:29,624 --> 00:25:32,829
Of course, Sam's career might eventually pay

739
00:25:32,829 --> 00:25:35,742
more, especially in a high paying field.

740
00:25:35,922 --> 00:25:38,716
Many college graduates do catch up and

741
00:25:38,716 --> 00:25:40,775
pass tradespeople over time.

742
00:25:40,955 --> 00:25:43,147
The point isn't that one path always

743
00:25:43,147 --> 00:25:43,451
wins.

744
00:25:43,631 --> 00:25:45,674
It's that the trades offer a head

745
00:25:45,674 --> 00:25:47,840
start that's easy to underestimate,

746
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:49,944
and if Alex later owns a business,

747
00:25:49,944 --> 00:25:51,863
the gap could grow much larger.

748
00:25:52,213 --> 00:25:54,999
Now add ownership to Alex's story.

749
00:25:55,179 --> 00:25:56,416
Say at thirty five,

750
00:25:56,416 --> 00:25:59,604
Alex starts an electrical contracting company and

751
00:25:59,604 --> 00:26:00,711
grows it to earn,

752
00:26:00,711 --> 00:26:02,012
in our illustration,

753
00:26:02,012 --> 00:26:04,811
around one hundred fifty thousand dollars a

754
00:26:04,811 --> 00:26:07,219
year in owner earnings by forty five.

755
00:26:07,399 --> 00:26:08,752
Twenty years later,

756
00:26:08,752 --> 00:26:11,030
Alex sells the company for a few

757
00:26:11,030 --> 00:26:12,739
times its yearly profit.

758
00:26:12,919 --> 00:26:15,092
That sale alone could be worth more

759
00:26:15,092 --> 00:26:17,202
than many office workers save in a

760
00:26:17,202 --> 00:26:17,761
lifetime.

761
00:26:18,111 --> 00:26:19,202
And what about Sam?

762
00:26:19,382 --> 00:26:21,240
If Sam becomes an engineer,

763
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:24,337
a nurse practitioner or a successful manager,

764
00:26:24,337 --> 00:26:26,470
Sam may earn more than Alex for

765
00:26:26,470 --> 00:26:27,915
much of their career.

766
00:26:28,095 --> 00:26:30,545
Many college paths pay off very well.

767
00:26:30,725 --> 00:26:32,800
The real lesson is that both paths

768
00:26:32,800 --> 00:26:33,837
can build wealth,

769
00:26:33,837 --> 00:26:36,217
and the trades deserve to be considered

770
00:26:36,217 --> 00:26:38,597
seriously, not dismissed as a fallback.

771
00:26:38,947 --> 00:26:41,633
The biggest variable in both stories isn't

772
00:26:41,633 --> 00:26:42,337
the career.

773
00:26:42,517 --> 00:26:44,301
It's what each person does with the

774
00:26:44,301 --> 00:26:44,607
money.

775
00:26:44,787 --> 00:26:47,075
A plumber who spends everything ends up

776
00:26:47,075 --> 00:26:49,129
with nothing, and so does a lawyer.

777
00:26:49,309 --> 00:26:52,205
A tradesperson who invests early and avoids

778
00:26:52,205 --> 00:26:54,563
debt can end up wealthier than many

779
00:26:54,563 --> 00:26:56,112
high earners who don't.

780
00:26:56,462 --> 00:26:58,422
Even if you love your office job,

781
00:26:58,422 --> 00:27:01,094
the trades teach some valuable money lessons.

782
00:27:01,274 --> 00:27:02,133
Lesson one.

783
00:27:02,313 --> 00:27:04,843
Avoid debt for credentials when there's a

784
00:27:04,843 --> 00:27:06,818
cheaper path to the same skills.

785
00:27:06,998 --> 00:27:11,156
Certifications, employer paid training and community colleges

786
00:27:11,156 --> 00:27:14,224
can sometimes deliver similar results for far

787
00:27:14,224 --> 00:27:14,974
less money.

788
00:27:15,324 --> 00:27:16,311
Lesson two.

789
00:27:16,491 --> 00:27:18,754
Build skills that are hard to replace.

790
00:27:18,934 --> 00:27:21,481
The trades are protected because the work

791
00:27:21,481 --> 00:27:22,847
is physical and local.

792
00:27:23,027 --> 00:27:24,192
In office jobs,

793
00:27:24,192 --> 00:27:26,290
the equivalent is judgment,

794
00:27:26,290 --> 00:27:30,251
relationships and expertise that AI and outsourcing

795
00:27:30,251 --> 00:27:31,572
struggle to copy.

796
00:27:31,752 --> 00:27:32,797
Lesson three.

797
00:27:32,977 --> 00:27:34,765
Look for a path to ownership.

798
00:27:34,945 --> 00:27:38,294
Many office workers can eventually start consulting

799
00:27:38,294 --> 00:27:41,052
practices or buy small businesses in their

800
00:27:41,052 --> 00:27:41,446
field.

801
00:27:41,797 --> 00:27:42,888
And lesson four.

802
00:27:43,068 --> 00:27:44,833
Get paid for extra effort.

803
00:27:45,013 --> 00:27:46,963
Tradespeople earn overtime.

804
00:27:47,143 --> 00:27:49,637
Office workers can look for bonuses,

805
00:27:49,637 --> 00:27:53,031
commissions, side consulting or roles where extra

806
00:27:53,031 --> 00:27:55,317
results translate into extra pay.

807
00:27:55,497 --> 00:27:57,786
Effort that isn't rewarded is a donation

808
00:27:57,786 --> 00:27:58,759
to your employer.

809
00:27:59,109 --> 00:28:00,154
Second angle.

810
00:28:00,334 --> 00:28:01,437
The psychology.

811
00:28:01,617 --> 00:28:03,764
Why do so many people overlook the

812
00:28:03,764 --> 00:28:04,206
trades?

813
00:28:04,556 --> 00:28:06,518
The first reason is status.

814
00:28:06,698 --> 00:28:09,778
For decades, schools and parents pushed college

815
00:28:09,778 --> 00:28:12,335
as the respectable path and treated the

816
00:28:12,335 --> 00:28:13,908
trades as a backup plan.

817
00:28:14,088 --> 00:28:15,651
That stigma is fading,

818
00:28:15,651 --> 00:28:16,433
but slowly.

819
00:28:16,613 --> 00:28:19,190
Many people still feel a blue collar

820
00:28:19,190 --> 00:28:20,551
job is a step down,

821
00:28:20,551 --> 00:28:22,197
even when it pays more.

822
00:28:22,547 --> 00:28:24,578
The second reason is invisibility.

823
00:28:24,758 --> 00:28:26,788
Office success is visible:

824
00:28:26,788 --> 00:28:29,286
titles, offices, LinkedIn posts.

825
00:28:29,466 --> 00:28:31,655
A plumber with a growing business and

826
00:28:31,655 --> 00:28:33,843
a paid off house doesn't announce it.

827
00:28:34,023 --> 00:28:36,122
The wealth in the trades is quiet,

828
00:28:36,122 --> 00:28:37,541
so people don't see it.

829
00:28:37,891 --> 00:28:40,352
The third reason is outdated information.

830
00:28:40,532 --> 00:28:42,564
Many parents' views of the trades are

831
00:28:42,564 --> 00:28:44,596
based on how things were decades ago.

832
00:28:44,776 --> 00:28:47,413
Today's trades involve technology,

833
00:28:47,413 --> 00:28:48,965
problem solving and,

834
00:28:48,965 --> 00:28:50,051
in many cases,

835
00:28:50,051 --> 00:28:53,077
strong pay and ownership opportunities.

836
00:28:53,427 --> 00:28:55,572
The fourth reason is the comfort of

837
00:28:55,572 --> 00:28:56,492
a clear script.

838
00:28:56,672 --> 00:28:58,878
College is a familiar path:

839
00:28:58,878 --> 00:29:00,840
apply, attend, graduate.

840
00:29:01,020 --> 00:29:03,200
The trades can feel less clear,

841
00:29:03,200 --> 00:29:05,522
with apprenticeship applications,

842
00:29:05,522 --> 00:29:08,476
union locals and licensing steps that many

843
00:29:08,476 --> 00:29:10,586
families have never navigated.

844
00:29:10,766 --> 00:29:12,337
Unfamiliar feels risky,

845
00:29:12,337 --> 00:29:13,634
even when it isn't.

846
00:29:13,984 --> 00:29:16,563
And the fifth reason is underestimating the

847
00:29:16,563 --> 00:29:17,223
brain work.

848
00:29:17,403 --> 00:29:19,980
People picture the trades as purely physical.

849
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:23,427
In reality, diagnosing an electrical fault or

850
00:29:23,427 --> 00:29:26,983
designing a plumbing system requires knowledge of

851
00:29:26,983 --> 00:29:29,959
codes, math, physics and problem solving.

852
00:29:30,139 --> 00:29:32,733
Many tradespeople are some of the sharpest

853
00:29:32,733 --> 00:29:34,772
problem solvers you'll ever meet.

854
00:29:35,122 --> 00:29:35,911
The takeaway.

855
00:29:36,091 --> 00:29:37,902
The trades aren't a backup plan.

856
00:29:38,082 --> 00:29:39,098
For many people,

857
00:29:39,098 --> 00:29:41,446
they're a front door to solid income,

858
00:29:41,446 --> 00:29:43,667
little debt and business ownership.

859
00:29:44,017 --> 00:29:44,864
Third angle.

860
00:29:45,044 --> 00:29:45,776
The playbook.

861
00:29:45,956 --> 00:29:48,115
How does someone actually get started?

862
00:29:48,465 --> 00:29:49,219
Step one.

863
00:29:49,399 --> 00:29:50,142
Explore.

864
00:29:50,322 --> 00:29:52,440
Talk to plumbers and electricians,

865
00:29:52,440 --> 00:29:54,184
visit a job site if you can,

866
00:29:54,184 --> 00:29:56,301
and try some basic projects to see

867
00:29:56,301 --> 00:29:57,672
if you enjoy the work.

868
00:29:57,852 --> 00:29:58,850
Step two.

869
00:29:59,030 --> 00:30:02,107
Look for apprenticeships through union locals,

870
00:30:02,107 --> 00:30:04,649
contractor associations and employers.

871
00:30:04,829 --> 00:30:08,174
Many list openings online and accept applications

872
00:30:08,174 --> 00:30:09,949
at specific times of year.

873
00:30:10,299 --> 00:30:11,297
Step three.

874
00:30:11,477 --> 00:30:14,749
Consider a pre apprenticeship or trade school

875
00:30:14,749 --> 00:30:17,584
program if you need basic skills first.

876
00:30:17,764 --> 00:30:18,855
Step four.

877
00:30:19,035 --> 00:30:22,134
Complete the apprenticeship and earn your journeyman

878
00:30:22,134 --> 00:30:22,611
license.

879
00:30:22,791 --> 00:30:23,697
Step five.

880
00:30:23,877 --> 00:30:26,738
Keep learning: specialties like solar,

881
00:30:26,738 --> 00:30:29,749
EV chargers, controls or medical gas can

882
00:30:29,749 --> 00:30:31,029
raise your value.

883
00:30:31,379 --> 00:30:32,447
Step six.

884
00:30:32,627 --> 00:30:34,252
Manage your money from day one.

885
00:30:34,432 --> 00:30:37,120
Save and invest part of every paycheck,

886
00:30:37,120 --> 00:30:40,841
especially during the apprenticeship when habits form.

887
00:30:41,021 --> 00:30:42,043
Step seven.

888
00:30:42,223 --> 00:30:43,839
If you want ownership,

889
00:30:43,839 --> 00:30:45,603
learn the business side:

890
00:30:45,603 --> 00:30:49,351
estimating, sales, bookkeeping and managing people.

891
00:30:49,531 --> 00:30:51,883
Many owners start with side work or

892
00:30:51,883 --> 00:30:54,303
buy a company from a retiring owner.

893
00:30:54,653 --> 00:30:55,802
And if you're older,

894
00:30:55,802 --> 00:30:56,835
it's not too late.

895
00:30:57,015 --> 00:30:59,266
Many people switch into the trades in

896
00:30:59,266 --> 00:31:00,847
their thirties or forties.

897
00:31:01,027 --> 00:31:03,540
Maturity and reliability are valued,

898
00:31:03,540 --> 00:31:06,332
and apprenticeships pay while you learn.

899
00:31:06,512 --> 00:31:08,420
The physical demands are real,

900
00:31:08,420 --> 00:31:09,947
so consider your health,

901
00:31:09,947 --> 00:31:12,236
but don't assume the door is closed.

902
00:31:12,586 --> 00:31:14,594
How do you choose between plumbing and

903
00:31:14,594 --> 00:31:15,175
electrical?

904
00:31:15,355 --> 00:31:18,766
Electrical work tends to involve more problem

905
00:31:18,766 --> 00:31:20,434
solving with circuits,

906
00:31:20,434 --> 00:31:22,026
codes and technology,

907
00:31:22,026 --> 00:31:23,087
and less mess.

908
00:31:23,267 --> 00:31:24,936
Plumbing involves water,

909
00:31:24,936 --> 00:31:25,979
drains and gas,

910
00:31:25,979 --> 00:31:27,022
can be messier,

911
00:31:27,022 --> 00:31:29,596
and often has a big emergency service

912
00:31:29,596 --> 00:31:30,291
component.

913
00:31:30,471 --> 00:31:32,898
Both pay similarly at the median.

914
00:31:33,078 --> 00:31:34,838
Try a day shadowing each,

915
00:31:34,838 --> 00:31:37,232
and choose the one you can imagine

916
00:31:37,232 --> 00:31:38,499
doing for decades.

917
00:31:38,850 --> 00:31:42,052
And don't forget related trades with similar

918
00:31:42,052 --> 00:31:44,163
advantages: HVAC technicians,

919
00:31:44,163 --> 00:31:46,274
elevator installers, welders,

920
00:31:46,274 --> 00:31:48,312
pipefitters and lineworkers.

921
00:31:48,492 --> 00:31:49,413
Some of these,

922
00:31:49,413 --> 00:31:51,190
like elevator installation,

923
00:31:51,190 --> 00:31:53,823
have even higher median pay according to

924
00:31:53,823 --> 00:31:55,863
the Bureau of Labor Statistics,

925
00:31:55,863 --> 00:31:58,233
though openings can be more limited.

926
00:31:58,583 --> 00:31:59,477
Question one.

927
00:31:59,657 --> 00:32:02,246
Do plumbers or electricians earn more?

928
00:32:02,426 --> 00:32:05,083
Median pay is very similar nationally,

929
00:32:05,083 --> 00:32:06,761
but it varies by region,

930
00:32:06,761 --> 00:32:08,649
specialty and union status.

931
00:32:08,829 --> 00:32:09,723
Question two.

932
00:32:09,903 --> 00:32:11,396
How long does it take to get

933
00:32:11,396 --> 00:32:11,876
licensed?

934
00:32:12,056 --> 00:32:14,938
Typically four to five years of apprenticeship,

935
00:32:14,938 --> 00:32:16,287
then a licensing exam,

936
00:32:16,287 --> 00:32:18,372
though requirements vary by state.

937
00:32:18,722 --> 00:32:19,709
Question three.

938
00:32:19,889 --> 00:32:21,328
Is college a waste of money?

939
00:32:21,508 --> 00:32:21,717
No.

940
00:32:21,897 --> 00:32:24,556
Many careers require it and pay well.

941
00:32:24,736 --> 00:32:27,446
The point is to compare paths honestly,

942
00:32:27,446 --> 00:32:29,531
including cost, debt and time.

943
00:32:29,711 --> 00:32:30,651
Question four.

944
00:32:30,831 --> 00:32:32,689
Can women succeed in the trades?

945
00:32:32,869 --> 00:32:33,832
Absolutely.

946
00:32:34,012 --> 00:32:35,967
Women are a growing part of the

947
00:32:35,967 --> 00:32:38,993
trades, and many programs actively recruit them.

948
00:32:39,343 --> 00:32:40,341
Question five.

949
00:32:40,521 --> 00:32:42,706
What about the physical toll as I

950
00:32:42,706 --> 00:32:42,971
age?

951
00:32:43,151 --> 00:32:45,850
Many tradespeople move into estimating,

952
00:32:45,850 --> 00:32:47,996
project management, inspection,

953
00:32:47,996 --> 00:32:51,248
teaching or business ownership in their forties

954
00:32:51,248 --> 00:32:52,079
and fifties.

955
00:32:52,259 --> 00:32:53,754
Planning for that shift,

956
00:32:53,754 --> 00:32:55,623
and saving aggressively early,

957
00:32:55,623 --> 00:32:57,367
makes the transition easier.

958
00:32:57,547 --> 00:32:58,685
Question six.

959
00:32:58,865 --> 00:33:00,057
Do I need to be good at

960
00:33:00,057 --> 00:33:00,316
math?

961
00:33:00,496 --> 00:33:01,560
Basic math helps,

962
00:33:01,560 --> 00:33:03,311
especially for electricians,

963
00:33:03,311 --> 00:33:05,814
but apprenticeships teach what you need.

964
00:33:06,164 --> 00:33:07,151
Question seven.

965
00:33:07,331 --> 00:33:09,199
Can I start a trade business without

966
00:33:09,199 --> 00:33:09,978
being a master?

967
00:33:10,158 --> 00:33:12,965
Most states require a master or contractor

968
00:33:12,965 --> 00:33:15,573
license to run a plumbing or electrical

969
00:33:15,573 --> 00:33:18,180
company, or to employ someone who holds

970
00:33:18,180 --> 00:33:18,447
one.

971
00:33:18,627 --> 00:33:20,701
Check your state's rules early,

972
00:33:20,701 --> 00:33:24,780
because licensing determines when ownership becomes possible.

973
00:33:25,131 --> 00:33:27,337
Let's recap the twelve advantages.

974
00:33:27,517 --> 00:33:28,875
Pay above the median.

975
00:33:29,055 --> 00:33:29,926
Paid to learn.

976
00:33:30,106 --> 00:33:31,952
Little or no student debt.

977
00:33:32,132 --> 00:33:33,502
A four year head start.

978
00:33:33,682 --> 00:33:35,899
Overtime and emergency pay.

979
00:33:36,079 --> 00:33:36,915
Side work.

980
00:33:37,095 --> 00:33:38,871
A clear path to ownership.

981
00:33:39,051 --> 00:33:41,350
Hard to automate or outsource.

982
00:33:41,530 --> 00:33:43,202
Demand from retirements.

983
00:33:43,382 --> 00:33:44,706
Union benefits.

984
00:33:44,886 --> 00:33:46,534
Lower costs of working.

985
00:33:46,714 --> 00:33:49,106
And skills that save money at home.

986
00:33:49,456 --> 00:33:51,255
And the honest tradeoffs:

987
00:33:51,255 --> 00:33:53,053
physical demands, cycles,

988
00:33:53,053 --> 00:33:55,284
schedules and variation in pay.

989
00:33:55,464 --> 00:33:57,276
The trades aren't for everyone,

990
00:33:57,276 --> 00:33:58,737
but for the right person,

991
00:33:58,737 --> 00:34:00,314
they can be one of the most

992
00:34:00,314 --> 00:34:02,418
reliable paths to wealth in America.

993
00:34:02,769 --> 00:34:04,697
So next time you see a plumber's

994
00:34:04,697 --> 00:34:06,926
van or an electrician's truck in your

995
00:34:06,926 --> 00:34:09,758
neighborhood, remember what might be behind it.

996
00:34:09,938 --> 00:34:12,340
Maybe a person who started earning at

997
00:34:12,340 --> 00:34:14,547
eighteen, never had student loans,

998
00:34:14,547 --> 00:34:17,208
invested early, and is quietly building a

999
00:34:17,208 --> 00:34:19,480
business and a net worth that would

1000
00:34:19,480 --> 00:34:21,687
surprise most people driving past.

1001
00:34:21,867 --> 00:34:23,850
Quiet wealth is still wealth,

1002
00:34:23,850 --> 00:34:25,013
and in many ways,

1003
00:34:25,013 --> 00:34:26,860
it's the most durable kind.

1004
00:34:27,210 --> 00:34:28,781
If you or someone you know is

1005
00:34:28,781 --> 00:34:29,648
choosing a path,

1006
00:34:29,648 --> 00:34:31,111
share this video with them.

1007
00:34:31,291 --> 00:34:32,212
And this week,

1008
00:34:32,212 --> 00:34:35,172
look up the apprenticeship programs near you,

1009
00:34:35,172 --> 00:34:37,014
just to see what's possible.

1010
00:34:37,194 --> 00:34:39,921
Many programs hold information sessions,

1011
00:34:39,921 --> 00:34:42,716
and some accept applications only once or

1012
00:34:42,716 --> 00:34:43,602
twice a year,

1013
00:34:43,602 --> 00:34:45,988
so knowing the dates early can save

1014
00:34:45,988 --> 00:34:46,738
you months.

1015
00:34:46,918 --> 00:34:48,871
And if you're already in the trades,

1016
00:34:48,871 --> 00:34:50,606
tell me in the comments what you

1017
00:34:50,606 --> 00:34:52,613
wish someone had told you about money

